NAME
       xargs - build command lines from standard input and run them

SYNOPSIS
       xargs [-0] [-n N] [-L N] [-I REPLACE] [-d DELIM] [-r] [-t] [COMMAND
             [INITIAL-ARGUMENTS]]

DESCRIPTION
       xargs reads words (separated by blanks or newlines) from standard
       input and runs COMMAND with them added as arguments, as few times as
       possible. It turns a list printed by one command into arguments for
       another: find ... | xargs rm. Without COMMAND it runs echo.

       File names with spaces break the default splitting. Pair find -print0
       with xargs -0, which splits on NUL bytes only.

OPTIONS
       -0, --null
              Items are separated by NUL bytes (from find -print0).

       -d, --delimiter=CHAR
              Items are separated by CHAR (for example -d '\n': one item per
              line).

       -n, --max-args=N
              Use at most N items per command run.

       -L, --max-lines=N
              Use at most N input lines per command run.

       -I REPLACE
              Run COMMAND once per input line, putting the line wherever
              REPLACE (often {}) appears.

       -r, --no-run-if-empty
              Do not run COMMAND at all when the input is empty.

       -t, --verbose
              Print each command line on standard error before running it.

       -p     Ask before running each command.

EXAMPLES
       Delete the .tmp files under /tmp/work, even with spaces in names:

           find /tmp/work -name '*.tmp' -print0 | xargs -0 rm -f

       Create the users listed in a file, one per line:

           xargs -n 1 useradd < /root/newusers.txt

       Copy each file named in a list into /root/backup:

           cat /root/list.txt | xargs -I {} cp {} /root/backup/

SEE ALSO
       find(1), bash(1)
